# TASK 02 – FROM REQUIREMENT TO HIERARCHY (8 MARKS)

## 2.1 Identify the Relationships (2 Marks)

The university system contains the following relationships:

1. **Person → Student:** Student is a Person. This is an is-a relationship and uses single inheritance.
2. **Person → Staff:** Staff is a Person. This is an is-a relationship and uses single inheritance.
3. **Student + Staff → TeachingAssistant:** A TeachingAssistant is both a Student and a Staff member. This is an is-a relationship and uses multiple inheritance.
4. **Student → ResearchScholar:** A ResearchScholar is a Student who also receives a fellowship. This is an is-a relationship and uses single inheritance.
5. **Fellowship:** Fellowship amount is stored as a data member in ResearchScholar, representing an attribute.

### Types of Inheritance Used

- Single inheritance – Person to Student and Person to Staff.
- Multiple inheritance – Student and Staff to TeachingAssistant.
- Hierarchical inheritance – Person is inherited by Student and Staff.
- Multilevel inheritance – Person → Student → ResearchScholar.
- Hybrid inheritance – Combination of hierarchical and multiple inheritance in the university hierarchy.

## 2.2 Resolve the Diamond Problem (2 Marks)

### Problem

When TeachingAssistant inherits from both Student and Staff, and both inherit from Person, TeachingAssistant gets two copies of the Person base class.

This creates the diamond problem:

```text
             Person
            /      \\
       Student     Staff
            \\      /
       TeachingAssistant
```

As a result, accessing common members such as name, ID, or phone may become ambiguous because two copies of Person exist.

### Solution: Virtual Base Classes

The diamond problem can be solved by using virtual inheritance.

```cpp
class Student : virtual public Person
{
};

class Staff : virtual public Person
{
};
```

By using virtual public Person, both Student and Staff share a single Person base-class subobject when inherited by TeachingAssistant.

### Diagram After Virtual Inheritance

```text
              Person
             /      \\
       (virtual)  (virtual)
          /          \\
      Student       Staff
          \\          /
       TeachingAssistant
```

Thus, TeachingAssistant contains only one copy of Person, eliminating ambiguity.

## 2.3 Design the Classes (3 Marks)

### C++ Class Declarations

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Person
{
protected:
    string name;
    int id;
    string phone;

public:
    Person(string n, int i, string p)
    {
        name = n;
        id = i;
        phone = p;
    }
};

class Student : virtual public Person
{
protected:
    string program;
    double cgpa;

public:
    Student(string n, int i, string p, string prog, double c)
        : Person(n, i, p)
    {
        program = prog;
        cgpa = c;
    }
};

class Staff : virtual public Person
{
protected:
    string department;
    double salary;

public:
    Staff(string n, int i, string p, string d, double s)
        : Person(n, i, p)
    {
        department = d;
        salary = s;
    }
};

class TeachingAssistant : public Student, public Staff
{
public:
    TeachingAssistant(string n, int i, string p,
                     string prog, double c,
                     string d, double s)
        : Person(n, i, p),
          Student(n, i, p, prog, c),
          Staff(n, i, p, d, s)
    {
    }
};

class ResearchScholar : public Student
{
private:
    double fellowship;

public:
    ResearchScholar(string n, int i, string p,
                   string prog, double c, double f)
        : Person(n, i, p),
          Student(n, i, p, prog, c)
    {
        fellowship = f;
    }
};
```

### Inheritance Decisions

| Class | Base Class | Inheritance Type |
|---|---|---|
| Student | Person | Virtual public |
| Staff | Person | Virtual public |
| TeachingAssistant | Student, Staff | Public multiple inheritance |
| ResearchScholar | Student | Public single inheritance |

**Reason:** Public inheritance represents an is-a relationship. Virtual inheritance is used for Student and Staff to avoid duplicate Person objects in TeachingAssistant.

## 2.4 Access Table (1 Mark)

The following table shows how members of Person are accessed inside Student under different inheritance modes.

| Member in Person | Public Inheritance | Protected Inheritance | Private Inheritance |
|---|---|---|---|
| Public | Public | Protected | Private |
| Protected | Protected | Protected | Private |
| Private | Not accessible | Not accessible | Not accessible |

**Explanation:** Private members of the base class cannot be directly accessed by the derived class. Public and protected members change their visibility according to the inheritance mode.

# PREDICT THE OUTPUT

### Given Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Person
{
    Person()
    {
        cout << "Person ";
    }
};

struct Student : virtual Person
{
    Student()
    {
        cout << "Student ";
    }
};

struct Staff : virtual Person
{
    Staff()
    {
        cout << "Staff ";
    }
};

struct TeachingAssistant : Student, Staff
{
    TeachingAssistant()
    {
        cout << "TA ";
    }
};

int main()
{
    TeachingAssistant ta;
    cout << '\\n';
}
```

### Predicted Output

```text
Person Student Staff TA
```

### Explanation

1. Person is constructed first because it is a virtual base class.
2. Student is constructed next.
3. Staff is constructed next.
4. TeachingAssistant is constructed last.

Since Person is inherited virtually by both Student and Staff, only one Person object is created.

### Remove Virtual and Predict the Output

If virtual is removed from both Student and Staff:

```cpp
struct Student : Person
{
};

struct Staff : Person
{
};
```

### New Output

```text
Person Student Person Staff TA
```

### Explanation

Without virtual inheritance, Student and Staff each contain their own Person subobject. Therefore, the Person constructor is called twice.

**Compiler messages:** The given program will still compile successfully. However, accessing a Person member such as ta.name would become ambiguous because TeachingAssistant would contain two Person subobjects.

# ALTERNATIVE CONCEPT AND COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS

## Alternative: Composition Instead of Multiple Inheritance

Instead of TeachingAssistant inheriting from Student and Staff, it can contain Student and Staff objects as members.

```cpp
class TeachingAssistant
{
private:
    Student student;
    Staff staff;

public:
    TeachingAssistant(Student s, Staff st)
        : student(s), staff(st)
    {
    }
};
```

Here, TeachingAssistant has-a Student and has-a Staff instead of being both a Student and Staff.

### Comparison

| Feature | Virtual Inheritance | Composition |
|---|---|---|
| Relationship | Is-a | Has-a |
| Person objects | One shared Person | Usually two Person subobjects |
| Space | Less duplication | May require more space |
| Accessing name | Direct access, unambiguous | Access through student/staff objects |
| Ambiguity | Avoided using virtual inheritance | Avoided using member access |
| Flexibility | Suitable for true is-a relationships | Suitable when objects are contained |

### Space Analysis Using sizeof

Virtual inheritance avoids duplicate Person subobjects in TeachingAssistant.

Composition may contain two separate Person subobjects—one inside Student and another inside Staff—so its object size may be larger.

The exact size can be measured using:

```cpp
cout << sizeof(TeachingAssistant);
```

The actual size depends on the compiler, padding, and implementation of virtual inheritance.

### Conclusion

Virtual inheritance is suitable when a TeachingAssistant is genuinely both a Student and a Staff member. It avoids duplication of the Person base class and provides a clean hierarchy. Composition is an alternative when TeachingAssistant only needs to contain Student and Staff objects rather than behave as both.
